WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:06.060
Undercover Boss is an incredibly popular CBS
reality show featuring CEOs or other high-ranking

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.360
executives donning disguises to work at the
lowest levels in their organization, all in

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:14.020
an effort to become better bosses — and
make the workplace a better place.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.480
With eight seasons and more than 100 episodes,
there's plenty of trivia out there about the

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.170
show and it's masked managers, but the show's
biggest fans have likely heard it all before.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.440
So here's some obscure Undercover Boss facts
for the die-hards.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.540
Undercover inspiration

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:33.220
It was while watching the real-life PR disaster
of the opening of British Airways Terminal

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:38.630
5 at Heathrow Airport in London in 2008 that
British reality TV producer Stephen Lambert

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:39.760
had a brilliant idea.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:44.440
According to Lambert's book, Undercover Boss:
Inside the TV Phenomenon That Is Changing

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.480
Bosses and Employees Everywhere, the kernel
of the concept for the show came from British

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.750
Airways head honcho Willie Walsh's response
to a reporter's question about whether he'd

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:56.360
suffered traveling inconveniences using the
very airline he runs.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.840
He responded, "I can't because people in BA
recognize me."

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:04.899
That simple truth led Lambert to pitch the
idea to Channel 4, and the first series aired

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:05.820
in 2009.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.640
Lambert and executive producer Eli Holzman
were then able to bring the show to CBS in

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:16.400
2010 and make it the American reality TV juggernaut
it is today, with one interesting difference:

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:20.920
in the British version, after the emotional
one-on-ones, the boss never reveals the ruse

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.960
in front of the entire company via a celebratory
screening of clips from the episode.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:29.200
Holzman says he thought Americans would want
a "big celebration," and the companies involved

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.580
have confirmed that to be true.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.220
Super start

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.680
The show's biggest fans know it's been a smash
since the very beginning: The first episode

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.800
of Undercover Boss in 2010 followed Super
Bowl 44, giving it the best possible lead-in.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.380
And while many shows have still failed in
the spot, Undercover Boss thrived, retaining

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.990
an astounding 38.6 million viewers for its
debut.

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:54.350
"One word, and that's just, 'Unbelievable.'"

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:59.460
Even after it moved to its regular 9PM Sunday
night slot, it still averaged 17 million viewers

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.780
per episode in its first season, making it
the most popular new show of the 2009-2010

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:05.480
television season.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.400
Mayor gets mired

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.780
Pittsburgh mayor Bill Peduto found himself
at the center of a controversy that he categorized

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.670
as "sublime ridiculousness" when County Controller
Chelsa Wagner refused to release taxpayer

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.560
funds to a tourist organization that offered
to donate some of the prize money awarded

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.440
to the city workers featured on the show.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:29.640
"You're going to be getting a check for $20,000
for the down payment, and the ability to purchase."

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:30.650
"Oh my God.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:31.560
Oh my G —"

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.840
Prior to his episode, Mayor Peduto promised
that no public funds would go towards the

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.840
prizes, but then VisitPittsburgh, an organization
"largely funded with taxpayer dollars," agreed

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:46.120
to donate $50,000 of "the $155,000 he pledged
to help four needy workers."

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.900
This prompted Wagner to withhold all taxpayer
funds from VisitPittsburgh, sparking a mini

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:55.370
controversy that died down a week later when
VisitPittsburgh clarified that their donations

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:59.290
came from their general fund, which is "a
mix of public and private dollars."

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:03.340
Wagner released the funds, but vowed further
action if it could be proven that tax dollars

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.340
were in fact used.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:08.720
Peduto had this to say on the matter: "Some
people want to use this as an opportunity

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:09.620
as a battering ram.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:13.840
I think it's the first time that any politician
has ever been accused for lining someone else's

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:14.340
pockets."

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.400
Undercover aftermath

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:23.490
One year after house-flipper-turned-seminar-guru
Armando Montelongo's episode aired, over 150

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:26.640
former students of his seminars filed a class
action lawsuit against him.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:30.840
Their suit alleges that the former Flip This
House host's seminar course, which purports

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:35.580
to instruct people on how to get paid to flip
houses, is a lie, and their widespread promotion

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.940
of that lie violates federal racketeering
laws.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.720
Among the accusations are bizarre claims that
Montelongo "studied a film about 'mind control

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.350
cults,' and used it to develop his programs,
and that advanced programs costing participants

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:52.400
up to $54,000 are nothing more than schemes
to get participants to keep buying additional

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.300
seminar products.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.920
Montelongo has refuted the allegations, telling
InTouch Weekly that the participants in the

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.560
lawsuit are people who "decided that continuous
hard work is not for them."

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:05.850
He also claims that some of them have started
rival seminars and are "colluding to come

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.920
together to try to bring down No. 1."

